### Step 1: Bug Scenario Images Analysis

**Image 1:**  
- Shows a menu item with a tooltip (title attribute) that duplicates the visible text.
- The tooltip appears even when the text is fully visible and not truncated.

**Image 2 & 3:**  
- Show a dropdown/select menu.
- When the item text is long and truncated (with ellipsis), the tooltip (title) shows the full text (expected and useful).
- When the item text is not truncated, the tooltip still appears, duplicating the visible text (undesirable per accessibility guidelines).

### Step 2: Bug Root Cause Reasoning

- The current implementation always sets the `title` attribute to the stringified children of the menu item, regardless of whether the text is truncated or not.
- This causes a redundant tooltip (title) to appear, violating accessibility guidelines (WCAG: "Do not repeat visible text in tooltips").
- The desired behavior is:
  - Only set `title` if the children are a pure string **and** the text is visually truncated (i.e., not fully visible).
  - If the children are not a pure string (e.g., contain icons or custom nodes), do **not** set the `title` by default.

### Step 3: Bug Code Snippets Analysis

#### `src/menu/view/item.jsx`
- The `render()` method always sets `title={this.getTitle(children)}`.
- The `getTitle()` method extracts all string content from children, regardless of truncation or node type.

#### `src/cascader/item.jsx`
- Uses `Menu.Item` and passes all props, so the fix in `Menu.Item` will propagate here.

### Step 4: Patch Generation

We need to:
- Only set the `title` attribute if the children are a pure string **and** (optionally) the text is truncated (for simplicity, we can start with the pure string check, as truncation detection is non-trivial in React without refs).
- If children are not a pure string, do **not** set the `title`.

